Parts that keep a permissive licence
------------------------------------

Some code is meant to run inside other people's websites, apps, servers and
plugins. It keeps a permissive licence, so that using it does not bring the
AGPL's terms into their software. A path's licence text is in its own LICENSE
file where it has one, and in LICENSES/ otherwise.

  Path                                       Licence     Why
  packages/server-sdk (@hamnivo/server)      MIT         Server SDK: runs inside a customer's own
                                                         server to sign identity assertions and
                                                         check webhooks.
  packages/client (@hamnivo/client)          MIT         Headless client SDK, bundled into a
                                                         customer's own web app.
  packages/react (@hamnivo/react)            MIT         React and Next.js bindings, published to
                                                         npm for customers' apps.
  packages/vue (@hamnivo/vue)                MIT         Vue and Nuxt bindings, published to npm for
                                                         customers' apps.
  packages/contracts (@hamnivo/contracts)    MIT         The public API's types, schemas and OpenAPI
                                                         description. The client SDK depends on it,
                                                         and anyone writing a client needs it.
  packages/widget-loader                     MIT         The one script a website adds to show the
                                                         chat launcher. It runs on the customer's
                                                         page.
  packages/domain/src/automations/index.ts   MIT         The widget loader bundles its browser rules
                                                         (browserMatches, deviceOf). The rest of
                                                         packages/domain is AGPL-3.0-only.
  integrations/php-sdk                       MIT         PHP server SDK, installed into customers'
                                                         PHP applications.
  integrations/wordpress                     MIT         WordPress plugin, installed into customers'
                                                         WordPress sites.
  integrations/whmcs                         MIT         WHMCS addon module, installed into
                                                         customers' WHMCS installations.
  integrations/shopify                       MIT         Shopify app and theme extension, installed
                                                         into customers' stores.
  integrations/gtm                           Apache-2.0  Google Tag Manager template. The Community
                                                         Template Gallery requires Apache 2.0.
  examples                                   MIT         Sample code meant to be copied into your own
                                                         site or server.

Everything else, including the gateway, Studio, the widget that runs in its
frame, the website, the documentation app and the shared packages they use, is
AGPL-3.0-only.


